# Web Game Quickstart

The whole path from an empty folder to a deployed, hosted browser game backed by a C# microservice and a Mongo microstorage. Each step names the skill with the details.

The worked example is a Rock-Paper-Scissors game: https://github.com/ali-beamable/TestGameBeamable (`services/MatchService`, `services/MatchStorage`, `web/`).

## When to Use This Skill
- The user wants a new browser (or Node) game or app that talks to Beamable
- The user wants server-authoritative game logic behind a web client

## Prerequisites
- .NET 10 SDK, Docker (to build service images when deploying), Node.js 22+
- The user's CID (or alias) and which realm to use

## Steps

### 1. Create the workspace
```
beam_exec("init --cid <CID> --realm <RealmName> -q")
```
Pass `--pid <PID>` instead of `--realm` if you know it, and `--game <GameName>` when several games have a realm with that name. Without a realm, quiet `init` picks the first game's oldest dev realm and prints which. Check it with `beam_exec("config -q")`. Details: `beam-initialize-project`.

### 2. Register the MCP server (optional)
```
beam_exec("mcp setup -q")
```
Writes `.mcp.json` with an absolute path to `dotnet`, restores the local `beam` tool and warns if `dotnet beam` doesn't start from the workspace.

### 3. Prepare the realm for the Web SDK
```
beam_exec("config realm check --for web --fix -q")
```
Sets the realtime publisher to `beamable` and publishes the `global` content manifest. Without these, `Beam.init` fails with `Unsupported websocket provider` or a 404 on the `global` manifest. Details: `beam-web-guide` → Realm prerequisites.

### 4. Create a storage and a service linked to it
```
beam_exec("project new storage MatchStorage -q")
beam_exec("project new service MatchService --link-to MatchStorage -q")
```
{{ if commands["project new service"] }}
