### 5. Write the endpoints and the stored document
A document lives in the storage project and extends `StorageDocument`:
```csharp
[BsonIgnoreExtraElements]
public class MatchRecord : StorageDocument
{
	public const string CollectionName = "matches";
	public long PlayerId;
	public string Status = "in_progress";
	public int PlayerWins;
	public int CpuWins;
}
```
The service gets the typed collection through the generated `Storage.<StorageName>Collection<T>(name)` extension, and uses `Context.UserId` for the calling player:
```csharp
public partial class MatchService : Microservice
{
	[ClientCallable]
	public async Task<MatchView> StartMatch()
	{
		var matches = await Storage.MatchStorageCollection<MatchRecord>(MatchRecord.CollectionName);
		var match = new MatchRecord { PlayerId = Context.UserId };
		await matches.InsertOneAsync(match);
		return MatchView.From(match);
	}

	[ClientCallable]
	public async Task<RoundResult> PlayRound(string matchId, string move) { /* ... */ }
}

[Serializable]
public class MatchView
{
	public string matchId;
	public string status;
	public int playerWins;
	public int cpuWins;
}
```
- Request/response DTOs use **public fields** and `[Serializable]`.
- `Promise<T>` is awaitable directly; `Task<T>` works too.
- Prefer `int`/`string` over `long` in DTOs the web client reads (a `long` becomes `bigint | string` in TypeScript unless you generate with `--int64-as`).
- Throw `MicroserviceException(status, error, message)` for client errors.

More patterns: `beam-microservice-examples`, `beam-manage-service`.

### 6. Build, then generate the TypeScript client
```
beam_exec("project build -q")
beam_exec("project generate web-client --output-dir web/src -q")
```
The generator reads `beam_openApi.json` from the build output, so build first (or pass `--build`). It writes `web/src/beamable/clients/MatchServiceClient.ts` and `web/src/beamable/clients/types/`.

### 7. Create the Vite + TypeScript client
```bash
npm create vite@latest web -- --template vanilla-ts
cd web && npm install @beamable/sdk
```
Set `base: './'` in `vite.config.ts` so the build works from any static path. Put the public identifiers in `web/.env`:
```
VITE_BEAM_CID=<CID>
VITE_BEAM_PID=<PID>
```
```ts
import { Beam } from "@beamable/sdk";
import { MatchServiceClient } from "./beamable/clients/MatchServiceClient";

// Signs the browser in as a guest (tokens persist), opens the realtime socket and sends session-start.
const beam = await Beam.init({
  cid: import.meta.env.VITE_BEAM_CID,
  pid: import.meta.env.VITE_BEAM_PID,
});
beam.use(MatchServiceClient);

const match = await beam.matchServiceClient.startMatch();
const round = await beam.matchServiceClient.playRound({ matchId: match.matchId, move: "rock" });
```
Details: `beam-web-guide`.

### 8. Deploy and wait until the services are running
```
beam_exec("deploy release --merge --wait -q")
```
`--merge` keeps remote services that aren't in this workspace; the default `--replace` removes them. `--wait` returns once every released service is running and current. Details: `beam-build-and-deploy`.

### 9. Verify
Play the game from the CLI and prove the realtime session works before trusting a browser:
```
beam_exec("project call MatchService StartMatch -q")
beam_exec("listen player --probe -q")
```
Details and a browser smoke test: `beam-test-and-verify`.

### 10. Host the client
Build with `npm run build` and publish `web/dist`, e.g. to GitHub Pages. Details: `beam-web-hosting`.

